    <h1 style="text-align:center; padding:20px">How Discovery Methods of Exoplanets Relate to Our Understanding of Their Features</h1>

    <!-- This page is for the discovery methods over time and discovery methods by facility -->

    <p>
        To understand how different discovery methods have shaped our knowledge of exoplanets,
        we first explored how the use of each method has changed over time. This helps establish
        which methods are most prevalent in the dataset and whether any particular era or event
        drove major shifts in discovery rates.
    </p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <div id="chart1"></div>
    </div>

    <p>
        Transit detection has dominated exoplanet discovery by a wide margin, accounting for
        over 4,400 of the confirmed planets in this dataset. Its rise was not gradual as discovery
        counts remained relatively steady through the 2000s before spiking dramatically, peaking
        at 1,431 planets discovered in 2016 alone. Radial velocity, the second most productive
        method with around 1,139 total discoveries, shows a much steadier trend without any
        single standout year. Microlensing has seen modest but consistent growth, particularly
        from 2020 onward, while imaging, astrometry, and pulsar timing remain rare throughout
        the entire timeline. The sharp spikes visible in transit discoveries suggest that
        individual missions, rather than broad community-wide effort, are driving most of the
        dataset.
    </p>

    <h2>Exploring the methods used by different discovery facilities</h2>

    <p>
        To dig deeper into which facilities are responsible for these discoveries, we looked at
        the top 15 facilities with the most discoveriesand broke down their output by discovery method.
        This reveals not just how many planets each facility has found, but whether facilities
        tend to specialize in a single method or employ multiple approaches.
    </p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <div id="chart2"></div>
    </div>

    <p>
        The Kepler space telescope is by far the most productive discovery facility, to the point
        it leaves all others in the dust on the original stacked bar chart, with over 2,700
        confirmed planets found exclusively through the transit method. TESS and K2, both also
        transit-focused missions, follow far behind in second and third place. Looking at the
        percentage breakdown, it becomes clear that most facilities rely almost entirely on a
        single discovery method. A few facilities, like Multiple Observatories and Paranal
        Observatory, show some spread across methods, but even then it is typically just two
        methods with one clearly dominant. The dropdown lets you highlight which facilities
        contribute to each method, making it easy to see that radial velocity is spread across
        several ground-based observatories while microlensing is concentrated almost entirely
        at KMTNet and OGLE.
    </p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <div id="chart3"></div>
    </div>

    <p>
        Filtering to 2013–2017 confirms that Kepler is responsible for the dramatic spikes
        seen in the time series above. In 2016 alone, Kepler contributed the vast majority
        of that year's record transit discoveries. The radio buttons allow comparison across
        individual years, making it clear that Kepler's dominance was not consistent since its
        output varied heavily year to year, which explains the jagged shape of the transit
        line in the earlier chart. Why Kepler produced so many more confirmations in certain
        years is not explained by this data alone, but likely reflects the timing of bulk
        confirmation campaigns run by the mission team rather than an actual change in
        discovery rate.
    </p>


